00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:28.140
Like say, OK, let's look at it
like I'm a classic developer.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:30.369
Maybe I run, I don't
know, a GC instance,

00:07:30.369 --> 00:07:32.910
like a virtual machine instance,
that has a web server on it,

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:34.830
and I'm going to go
access some data.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:38.890
How does GCP security
and encryption--

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:40.850
how does that help
me from the requests

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.680
coming in through to
accessing the data?

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.440
Start at the top-- what happens?

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:47.550
UMESH: Yeah, that's
a great question

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:51.000
because I think what's different
about some of what we're

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.770
talking about today than some
of the previous podcast topics

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:55.780
is that we're talking
about a little bit more

00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:59.790
of what goes on under the
hood versus the external

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:02.750
developer-facing part of GCP.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:05.230
So one of the things
that GCP offers

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:07.830
is the ability to store data.

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:09.640
And we want to make
it safe for you

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:11.170
to store even sensitive data.

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:15.374
So in your hypothetical scenario
where you have a web server,

00:08:15.374 --> 00:08:17.040
some of the content
you might be serving

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.650
could be private-- might
not be all public content.

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:21.380
And you really
want to be careful

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:23.740
that that data is
protected at rest,

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:26.790
meaning, like, when it's
stored in some system.

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:29.290
And we offer a variety of
storage products in GCP

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:30.510
that let you do that.

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:34.200
So Cloud Storage is
a classic example.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.940
Now, the way that systems
like Cloud Storage

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:40.220
are actually implemented
tends to be these big software

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:40.919
stacks.

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:44.770
It's not just, like, one process
in a disk somewhere, right?

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:48.200
It tends to be a whole set of
different pieces of software.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:53.070
So you send your request
to an API like the GCS API,

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:57.210
and you make a request to,
say, read or write piece

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:59.330
a piece of content in a bucket.

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:02.010
And that goes through a lot
of different processing.

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:04.020
So we decide, like,
OK, well, who are you?

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:07.430
Are you allowed to
do this operation?

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:08.676
What's your quota like?

00:09:08.676 --> 00:09:10.550
We have to do building,
we have to figure out

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:12.230
where that data is located.

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:15.560
We probably have it replicated
in a bunch of different places.

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:18.720
And so all that happens
through a sequence of servers

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:20.430
that the request touches.

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:23.960
A lot of it's built on our
own internal infrastructure,

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:25.660
and even that tends
to have layers.

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:27.990
So we'll have our own
internal storage API,

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:30.320
and below that, we'll have
a low-level thingy that

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:32.300
manages the disks, right?

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:35.360
And so in the course of
this request being served,

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.810
a lot of different
things happen.

00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:42.560
And we actually use different
kinds of security technologies

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:45.430
at every level of that stack.

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:49.710
So at each hop, as one
service talks to another,

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:53.620
we transmit information on
who is making the request,

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:55.750
and we try to use strong
forms of authentication

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:58.000
to make sure we know
really who that was

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.090
and that we're
not just guessing.

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:03.970
We also use, extensively,
access controls.

00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:06.220
So we say, all
right, you're trying

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:07.820
to make a request to GCS.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:11.460
We check your IAM policy, for
example, to say, OK, yeah,

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:12.630
were you allowed to do that?

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:15.710
But even internally to Google
when that service talks

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:18.500
to another service, we
make sure that data only

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:23.290
flows along correct paths for
specific kinds of requests.

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:26.570
At various points in the
stack, we use encryption.

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:31.880
And encryption is something
that we throw around

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:34.030
a lot because it's this
really important tool

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:38.220
in our toolbox for security,
but it is still just a tool.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:39.860
And one of the most
important things

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:42.030
that encryption lets
us do is it lets

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:44.770
us reason about what
parts of the stack

00:10:44.770 --> 00:10:46.980
we trust to do what things.

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:50.250
So if you think about
this deep software stack

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:53.940
where data is flowing down
and data is flowing up,

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:56.550
ideally, you don't
want to fully have

00:10:56.550 --> 00:11:01.530
to trust all layers of the stack
with access to all the data.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:04.860
And so what you can do at
some layer is say, OK, I'm

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.110
going to encrypt the data
before I put it in the lower

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:09.120
layer, and that
way, I don't have

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.400
to fully trust that
lower layer of the stack

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:13.650
because all I can see
is the encrypted data.

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.250
I don't have to worry
as much about exposure

00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:19.270
via administrative controls
or via bugs or hacks

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:21.010
or whatever it might be.

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:21.510
And

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:23.240
So this is one
way that we employ

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:25.750
what we call defense in depth.

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:28.540
We have multiple layers
of encryption that we do,

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:30.770
whether it's at the
storage API level.

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:33.640
We also do it again at
the physical disk level

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:36.860
so that even if there is
a bug or a problem at one

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:40.630
level of the stack, we tend
to have more layers of defense

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:43.985
so that the chance that your
data will ever be in the clear

00:11:43.985 --> 00:11:48.101
or actually be exposed is
reduced as much as possible.

00:11:48.101 --> 00:11:48.726
FRANCESC: Cool.

00:11:48.726 --> 00:11:53.430
So basically what that means
is that the lower levels don't

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:55.780
get to see your actual data.

00:11:55.780 --> 00:12:00.940
Does that mean that as a
Google Cloud Storage user,

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:03.479
should I encrypt my data, or
does that happen automatically?

00:12:03.479 --> 00:12:05.020
UMESH: Yeah, that's
a great question.

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:09.580
I think it really
depends on your use case.

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:12.840
And I think some
amount of history

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:17.190
is probably useful to
talk about at this point.

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:21.480
About 2007-2008,
we really embarked

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:25.290
on this big project, which
we called our data protection

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:25.900
effort.

00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:27.550
And one of the first
things we built

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:29.430
is what we call a key
management system,

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:33.800
so that lets you provision
cryptographic keys

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:36.070
and use them to do
things like encrypt data

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:39.960
at rest, among lots of other
applications you might do.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.200
And so we built this
thing, and we said,

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:44.220
hey, we have some
applications that have

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:46.060
really, really sensitive data.

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:47.950
Let's use this key
management system

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:52.030
to access encryption
keys, encrypt some data,

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:55.600
and just how we talked about,
reduce the amount of trust we

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:57.050
have to [INAUDIBLE]
in other parts

00:12:57.050 --> 00:13:00.690
of our system and our platform.

00:13:00.690 --> 00:13:03.110
And so we did that,
and it turns out

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:07.680
that managing keys is
really a lot of work.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:09.780
For the average
application developer,

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:14.150
having to think about stuff like
what kind of keys should I use?

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:16.400
What encryption
mode should I use?

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:18.140
When should I rotate keys?

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:19.980
When should I re-encrypt data?

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:23.480
All these kind of questions
that come up on an ongoing basis

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:25.830
is just really, really painful.

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:29.502
And they'd rather just focus
on application logic, right?

00:13:29.502 --> 00:13:30.960
I mean, that's kind
of a philosophy

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:33.520
that we bring to all of
Cloud Platform, right?

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:36.320
We want you not to have
to worry about a lot

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:40.272
of routine maintenance
reliability kind of tasks

00:13:40.272 --> 00:13:42.730
and let you focus on the things
that you really want to do,

00:13:42.730 --> 00:13:42.970
right?

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:44.594
What's the really
differentiating thing

00:13:44.594 --> 00:13:47.050
about your app?

00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:51.410
So after trying this kind of
everyone-for-themselves kind

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:53.730
of strategy, we
realized we needed

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:56.860
to have a more
integrated solution.

00:13:56.860 --> 00:14:00.800
And so what we did is we did
a really big project to build

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:04.590
in encryption at rest,
and at the same time,

00:14:04.590 --> 00:14:07.130
a bunch of other strong
operational security

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:10.952
controls to all our
core storage offerings.

00:14:10.952 --> 00:14:12.660
So a lot of these have
now made their way

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:16.000
into GCP-- things like
Big Table and Spanner

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:20.200
and things behind GCS, as well.

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:24.230
And by integrating those
deep security features

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:27.330
like encryption, like
we actually verified

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:31.900
binaries so we know exactly
what code and configuration is

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:33.660
running in our
production network that's

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:35.495
ever allowed to touch keys.

00:14:35.495 --> 00:14:37.120
That's really important
because then we

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:40.370
can trace that all the way
back to our source code system.

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:43.430
We have auditing controls
in the rare instances where

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:47.110
a person ever needs to
touch the sensitive material

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:49.000
for debugging, for example.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.760
We have audit logs
of all that stuff.

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:54.160
So that kind of stuff we
kind of built all of a piece,

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:56.270
and what that let
us do is say, OK,

00:14:56.270 --> 00:14:59.420
if you're a regular
application developer, which

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:02.110
is to say like most
application developers--

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:04.720
Google has a wide
variety of teams

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:07.490
making a wide variety
of applications-- a lot

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:10.370
of that work is not something
you need to actively worry

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:11.570
about anymore.

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:14.170
You just use the core storage
and you get all those features

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:15.520
for free.

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:18.242
That said, there
are still some cases

00:15:18.242 --> 00:15:19.700
where we'll want
to do more or they

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:21.700
have somewhat different needs.

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:25.110
So examples would
be things like you

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:28.010
need to send data externally.

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:31.450
So you need to send a piece of
encrypted content out to a user

00:15:31.450 --> 00:15:33.770
or to a partner
or something else.

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:36.220
Cases like that,
just having exception

00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:38.840
at rest that's hidden by storage
isn't good enough, right?

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:41.140
You need to agree
on what kind of keys

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:44.024
you're using, what the encrypted
data formats look like,

00:15:44.024 --> 00:15:45.690
and you can transmit
it to somebody else

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:48.330
or send it out there and then
get it back and interpret

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:50.110
the data.

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:52.180
So that would be one
example of a case

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:54.310
where you'd want
to do it yourself.

00:15:54.310 --> 00:15:57.220
In some other examples, just the
nature of your infrastructure

00:15:57.220 --> 00:15:58.280
is different.

00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:00.910
Like, it doesn't look
like regular storage.

00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:03.310
You want to do something
with a custom kind of index,

00:16:03.310 --> 00:16:04.526
for example.

00:16:04.526 --> 00:16:06.400
And those are also cases
where you might need

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:08.330
to manage your own encryption.

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:11.760
But those cases we found are
relatively few and far between,

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:14.860
and they do have really
large teams that are actively

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:16.970
staffed to manage that stuff.

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:21.590
So my expectation would be that
people using Cloud Platform

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:24.030
are going to follow a
similar distribution.

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:27.130
That most people should
be able to just rely

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:31.244
on the very strong security
guarantees that we give you,

00:16:31.244 --> 00:16:32.910
but that there is
going to be people who

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:34.830
have particular
requirements, or maybe they

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:37.960
have that driven by compliance
or external compatibility,

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:39.120
for example.

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:42.850
And in those cases, people may
want to manage their own keys.

00:16:42.850 --> 00:16:44.990
Or in the most
extreme cases, people

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:47.490
might want to say,
look, you guys at Google

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:50.500
are great and everything,
but, you know,

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:52.130
my stuff is super,
super sensitive.

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:54.360
I'm willing to invest
a lot of effort.

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:58.660
I don't want Google to ever
see the plaintext data.

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:01.350
And that's fine, too.

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:04.377
There are people that use
Cloud Platform in that way.

00:17:04.377 --> 00:17:05.960
And it's something
that we want to do,

00:17:05.960 --> 00:17:08.940
is give people a
spectrum of options.

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:12.960
So another option between
those two extremes

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:16.109
is actually a feature that we
launched with Compute Engine

00:17:16.109 --> 00:17:18.810
and Cloud Storage called
customer-supplied encryption

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:22.900
keys, where you can actually
generate the key on your own

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:25.099
and supply it along
with your API call,

00:17:25.099 --> 00:17:27.780
but Google will take care of
the actual mechanics of doing

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:29.550
the encryption for you.

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:31.580
MARK: Basically,
encryption is a continuum.

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:33.600
UMESH: Yeah.

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:35.600
FRANCESC: Maybe this is
the name of the episode.

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:36.100
Who knows?

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:45.210
OK, so in a nutshell,
basically what we have is

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:48.330
we can go from I
don't do anything,

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:50.510
and I get protected
because there

00:17:50.510 --> 00:17:52.990
is all of those
mechanisms implemented

00:17:52.990 --> 00:17:56.600
behind the curtains.

00:17:56.600 --> 00:17:59.480
But at the same time, if
I need more protection

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:02.200
or I want to have something
more specific because I have

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.265
my own needs, there's
also the possibility

00:18:04.265 --> 00:18:05.367
of implementing that.

00:18:05.367 --> 00:18:06.500
Cool.

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:08.160
MARK: OK, so that's really cool.

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:11.300
It sounds like we have
a variety of options.

00:18:11.300 --> 00:18:13.089
So if I have like,
I don't know, I just

00:18:13.089 --> 00:18:15.130
want to put my photos up
there and I don't really

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:17.409
care who sees them, I can
do that really easily.

00:18:17.409 --> 00:18:19.950
But if I've got something that
I don't want the world to see,

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:22.200
I can do that, as well.

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:25.340
But I suppose it's worth sort
of digging into it, as well.

00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:28.080
So it's great that
GCP does encryption,

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:30.332
and encryption standards
are there for you,

00:18:30.332 --> 00:18:32.790
and you're able to, kind of,
pull and push them in the ways

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:33.640
you want.

00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:37.630
But how does that really
fit into the whole security

00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:38.640
big picture?

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:40.817
Like, is this just
all I need to do,

00:18:40.817 --> 00:18:43.150
or do I need to worry about
more things other than that?

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:45.310
We talked a little bit
about authentication.

00:18:45.310 --> 00:18:47.420
Where does that fit in?

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:50.000
UMESH: That's a
really good question.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.540
So as we talked about
earlier, encryption

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:54.620
is not a silver bullet.

00:18:54.620 --> 00:18:59.570
It is a tool, and it's a tool
that, if you use it properly,

00:18:59.570 --> 00:19:03.080
can really improve
your security story.

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:05.740
So it might be worth just
stepping back for a second

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:07.790
and saying, what
do you actually get

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:11.930
from using cryptography
in the sense

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:14.690
that we care about it for GCP?

00:19:14.690 --> 00:19:19.660
The two properties that
people most look to crypto for

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:23.492
are called confidentiality
and integrity.

00:19:23.492 --> 00:19:25.960
So confidentiality is what
people mostly think of,

00:19:25.960 --> 00:19:27.810
which is if something
is encrypted,

00:19:27.810 --> 00:19:30.180
then unless you
have the key, you

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:32.290
can't figure out what it is.

00:19:32.290 --> 00:19:36.270
There are actually very
strict, rigorous definitions

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:40.080
of exactly what that means,
but more or less, that's

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:41.660
the intuition.

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:45.380
The other thing that you
want to get is integrity.

00:19:45.380 --> 00:19:47.950
And integrity is
about making sure

00:19:47.950 --> 00:19:51.010
that the data can't
be changed unless you

00:19:51.010 --> 00:19:53.770
have a particular key.

00:19:53.770 --> 00:19:56.364
And those two things--
actually, sometimes you

00:19:56.364 --> 00:19:58.280
might think they're the
same, but they're not.

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:00.010
There actually
are crypto systems

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:03.326
where you can have
great confidentiality--

00:20:03.326 --> 00:20:06.680
sort of under certain
reasonable assumptions,

00:20:06.680 --> 00:20:10.020
no one can know what the
encrypted data actually relates

00:20:10.020 --> 00:20:12.780
to as far as the clear data.

00:20:12.780 --> 00:20:16.456
But it is also possible to
change that in such a way

00:20:16.456 --> 00:20:17.830
that it has a
predictable result.

00:20:17.830 --> 00:20:20.510
So if I encrypted
a number, someone

00:20:20.510 --> 00:20:23.270
could change it in a way
that when I decrypt it,

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:25.220
I actually got twice the
original number, even

00:20:25.220 --> 00:20:27.590
though they didn't know
what that number was.

00:20:27.590 --> 00:20:30.650
So it is important to do
things in a careful way

00:20:30.650 --> 00:20:33.500
that you get both
confidentiality and integrity,

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:35.170
and that's the best
practice, and that's

00:20:35.170 --> 00:20:38.170
something that we strive to do.

00:20:40.420 --> 00:20:41.920
And people who are
really interested

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:45.840
in the very particulars of
the details of how GCP does

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:48.060
these things, by the
way, should definitely

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:50.790
check out the white
paper that we recently

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:54.780
released, talking in quite a
bit of detail about exactly

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:58.370
where we employ what types
of encryption and what modes

00:20:58.370 --> 00:21:01.020
and what kind of data is covered
and all that kind of stuff.

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:04.540
So please do check that out.

00:21:04.540 --> 00:21:07.930
So anyway, to come back to
the basic purpose of crypto

00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:10.760
and how it relates
overall security,

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:15.270
one way that I like to
think about using encryption

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:19.160
of data at rest is it
gives you a long lever

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:22.180
for access control.

00:21:22.180 --> 00:21:24.290
What I mean by that
is a lot of times,

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:27.170
you're managing a really
large amount of data.

00:21:27.170 --> 00:21:29.930
And there are certain
things you can

00:21:29.930 --> 00:21:31.950
do with a small
amount of data that

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:34.920
are not as easy or
practical or efficient to do

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:36.730
with a lot of data.

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:38.710
And what you get with
crypto is you can say,

00:21:38.710 --> 00:21:41.070
all right, none of
this data is accessible

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:42.800
unless I have this key.

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:45.230
So if I'm able to
protect the key, which

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:48.540
I can do lots of special
or sophisticated things

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:50.630
to protect that
key, that's actually

00:21:50.630 --> 00:21:53.560
equivalent to protecting this
much larger amount of data

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:55.140
that it may not be
as practical to do

00:21:55.140 --> 00:21:57.310
those same techniques with it.

00:21:57.310 --> 00:21:59.730
So examples of the kind
of things you can do

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:04.220
are things like keep
keys only in RAM

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:06.040
so you don't ever
persist them, and that

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:07.790
gives you a certain
measure of protection.

00:22:07.790 --> 00:22:09.750
So if, like, power
goes off, keys

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:11.980
are gone-- that's kind
of a nice property you

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:13.610
might want to have.

00:22:13.610 --> 00:22:17.650
You can use very special
machines that are really,

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:19.820
really locked down,
and they're only

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:23.180
for this special purpose of
doing high-security operations

00:22:23.180 --> 00:22:24.940
and managing keys.

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:27.004
You can't really do
that for all your data

00:22:27.004 --> 00:22:28.670
because at that point,
you're not really

00:22:28.670 --> 00:22:30.253
locking it down at
all if everything's

00:22:30.253 --> 00:22:31.950
within that same boundary.

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:35.470
You can have a dedicated
team of security specialists

00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:37.900
that manage just the key
infrastructure and just

00:22:37.900 --> 00:22:39.730
the libraries,
and you can really

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:42.360
focus a lot of your effort
of making those really good,

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:44.610
which, again, is
hard to scale to pay

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:48.400
that much attention, that amount
of care, to every single thing

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:49.960
that you do.

00:22:49.960 --> 00:22:53.750
So you can kind think
of crypto as a lever.

00:22:53.750 --> 00:22:56.850
And if you really are careful
about who can access the keys,

00:22:56.850 --> 00:22:59.060
that gives you a certain
measure of confidence around

00:22:59.060 --> 00:23:03.810
who can access that much
larger amount of data.

00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:07.290
But it doesn't solve all
your problems, right?

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:11.430
It doesn't solve the problems
of, for example, auditing

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:13.660
is another tool
that we like to use.

00:23:13.660 --> 00:23:15.920
[INAUDIBLE] It is
the case that, like,

00:23:15.920 --> 00:23:18.680
a person may need to
access a piece of data

00:23:18.680 --> 00:23:22.250
for administrative or
debugging purposes.

00:23:22.250 --> 00:23:25.450
That's part of what you need to
do to keep a platform running.

00:23:25.450 --> 00:23:29.080
And that means
that that person in

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:32.080
certain limited circumstances
has legitimate access.

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:34.546
So indirectly-- you
generally don't give them

00:23:34.546 --> 00:23:37.170
the key directly, you give them
access to a system which itself

00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:39.170
has access to the
key, but the point is,

00:23:39.170 --> 00:23:40.730
they're able to use it.

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:42.340
And so just relying
on the crypto

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:44.080
doesn't give you
everything you need.

00:23:44.080 --> 00:23:46.730
You oftentimes want to go
back and look at, OK, well,

00:23:46.730 --> 00:23:50.080
what did that person do
with that special access?

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:52.840
Did they use their access
properly and correctly

00:23:52.840 --> 00:23:55.180
for the purpose that
it was intended?

00:23:55.180 --> 00:23:58.840
So that doesn't magically
solve that problem for you.

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:02.760
A related problem is
processing that goes on

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:05.640
with data happens
in the background

00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:06.980
or it happens in batch.

00:24:06.980 --> 00:24:09.400
So for example,
let's say you store

00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:11.790
a lot of data in
a database, and we

00:24:11.790 --> 00:24:14.210
want to build an index on
that data so when you search,

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:17.454
you get really,
really quick results.

00:24:17.454 --> 00:24:19.620
That needs to operate on
the data in the background,

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:21.340
and therefore, needs access.

00:24:21.340 --> 00:24:24.500
So again, indirectly it
may be able to use data

00:24:24.500 --> 00:24:27.450
that is normally encrypted.

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:30.070
So the crypto
doesn't protect you

00:24:30.070 --> 00:24:33.790
intrinsically from that
code being the wrong code

00:24:33.790 --> 00:24:36.950
or code that does something
inappropriate, so you

00:24:36.950 --> 00:24:38.620
need other security mechanisms.

00:24:38.620 --> 00:24:40.480
So as I was saying
before, we've actually

00:24:40.480 --> 00:24:43.730
built stuff which makes
sure that, for things that

00:24:43.730 --> 00:24:46.390
touch sensitive data
in context like that,

00:24:46.390 --> 00:24:49.560
it's possible to tie it all
the way back to the [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:53.330
source code that that binary
came from, like the indexing

00:24:53.330 --> 00:24:55.800
code in this case,
and it uses, actually,

00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:58.850
a secure build in
release pipeline

00:24:58.850 --> 00:25:03.050
so we can actually say, yeah,
only that indexing code ever

00:25:03.050 --> 00:25:05.230
touched this piece of data.

00:25:05.230 --> 00:25:07.550
And there's a variety of
other mechanisms that we use.

00:25:07.550 --> 00:25:10.410
We actually have a
hardened platform

00:25:10.410 --> 00:25:14.280
that goes all the way from
we do supply chain management

00:25:14.280 --> 00:25:15.360
and we build our bios.

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:17.540
We do all kinds of
stuff, which hopefully we

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:20.370
can talk about in a future
episode in a lot more detail.

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:22.350
But just want to give
a little bit of context

00:25:22.350 --> 00:25:25.870
on well, what does
encryption buy you?

00:25:25.870 --> 00:25:30.392
And coming back to
the point of what's

00:25:30.392 --> 00:25:31.600
the deal with key management?

00:25:31.600 --> 00:25:32.900
Like, why is this hard?

00:25:32.900 --> 00:25:34.900
Well, a lot of
crypto boils down to

00:25:34.900 --> 00:25:40.250
not just is the crypto strong,
but who can access the key?

00:25:40.250 --> 00:25:43.390
And I think an interesting
physical analogy

00:25:43.390 --> 00:25:49.390
is let's say that you made a
car with an unbreakable lock.

00:25:49.390 --> 00:25:51.950
So unless you had a
key that fit that lock,

00:25:51.950 --> 00:25:55.430
there's just no way to
get in that car, right?

00:25:55.430 --> 00:25:58.180
So that's the equivalent
of really strong crypto.

00:25:58.180 --> 00:26:01.020
So if you were to use something
like that, you [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:01.020 --> 00:26:06.256
really, really sure you never
locked your key in the car

00:26:06.256 --> 00:26:07.880
because if you did,
you would just have

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:09.780
to send the car to be crushed.

00:26:09.780 --> 00:26:11.780
That would be it for the car.

00:26:11.780 --> 00:26:13.100
You would lose it.

00:26:13.100 --> 00:26:15.194
And so that's the
same kind of thing

00:26:15.194 --> 00:26:16.860
that you do if you
manage your own keys,

00:26:16.860 --> 00:26:20.359
is you need to make
sure you never lose it.

00:26:20.359 --> 00:26:21.900
And you also need
to make sure if you

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:23.483
make a copy because
someone else needs

00:26:23.483 --> 00:26:26.650
to get in there once in a
while, that that copy doesn't

00:26:26.650 --> 00:26:29.050
hang around.

00:26:29.050 --> 00:26:31.520
And that's challenging, too.

00:26:31.520 --> 00:26:34.350
And so in the case
of cars, of course,

00:26:34.350 --> 00:26:36.951
like we make a different
security usability trade-off,

00:26:36.951 --> 00:26:37.450
right?

00:26:37.450 --> 00:26:40.033
We make sure it's not too hard
to get in so you can still call

00:26:40.033 --> 00:26:41.920
AAA if your car breaks down.

00:26:41.920 --> 00:26:44.550
In the case of sensitive
data, what you really want

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:46.000
is strong access controls.

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:48.460
You want to make sure that only
the appropriate [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:51.390
systems can access data
at the appropriate times.

00:26:51.390 --> 00:26:53.744
And crypto is one tool
we use to do this,

00:26:53.744 --> 00:26:55.910
and we try to make it as
easy as possible for people

00:26:55.910 --> 00:26:58.520
by taking care of all
those details around well,

00:26:58.520 --> 00:27:00.170
who gets the keys
when, and how do we

00:27:00.170 --> 00:27:02.482
pull them back, and
so forth, so that you

00:27:02.482 --> 00:27:03.690
don't have to do it yourself.

00:27:03.690 --> 00:27:06.780
MARK: So as a Googler, I guess,
then, it means so I can't just

00:27:06.780 --> 00:27:09.330
go into the Cloud Platform
stuff and be like,

00:27:09.330 --> 00:27:11.030
oh, I'm just going
to grab your data,

00:27:11.030 --> 00:27:13.160
because I've got no access
to any of those keys,

00:27:13.160 --> 00:27:14.050
even if I could somehow
get into that stuff.

00:27:14.050 --> 00:27:14.200
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:14.332
UMESH: That's right, Yeah.

00:27:14.332 --> 00:27:16.415
We do try to maintain
really tight access controls

00:27:16.415 --> 00:27:17.400
on the data.

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:21.090
And we have audit logging
for when people ever

00:27:21.090 --> 00:27:25.600
need to access the data,
which, again, is not common.

00:27:25.600 --> 00:27:27.920
And we also maintain
a separation

00:27:27.920 --> 00:27:30.262
of administrative domains
between the people

00:27:30.262 --> 00:27:32.220
that maintain the key
management service, which

00:27:32.220 --> 00:27:34.540
maintains the encryption
keys, and the people that

00:27:34.540 --> 00:27:36.530
maintain, like, the
application code

00:27:36.530 --> 00:27:38.470
or the other infrastructure.

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:41.260
So that kind of helps
mitigate our risk, as well.

00:27:41.260 --> 00:27:42.385
FRANCESC: Very interesting.

00:27:42.385 --> 00:27:44.780
That really sounds
like managing keys

00:27:44.780 --> 00:27:48.260
is such a complicated thing that
the fact that Google is doing

00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:51.454
it instead of having you
as an application developer

00:27:51.454 --> 00:27:52.370
to take care of that--

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:54.910
UMESH: Yeah, no one
should want to do that.

00:27:54.910 --> 00:27:57.510
FRANCESC: I'm at least very
happy I don't have to do that.

00:27:57.510 --> 00:27:59.175
MARK: I've had to
work on applications

00:27:59.175 --> 00:28:02.030
that did that stuff, and
it is really, really hard

00:28:02.030 --> 00:28:03.610
to get it right.

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:06.190
And this is what I
spend my time doing.

00:28:06.190 --> 00:28:08.050
So I think that
should definitely

00:28:08.050 --> 00:28:12.950
be kind of like your
second option, let's say.

00:28:12.950 --> 00:28:13.770
FRANCESC: Nice.

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:15.410
So we're kind of
running out of time,

00:28:15.410 --> 00:28:17.530
but there was one
more topic that I

00:28:17.530 --> 00:28:20.370
would like to mention just
because it's so fancy,

00:28:20.370 --> 00:28:23.550
and it especially has a
very fancy name, which

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:26.130
is homomorphic encryption.

00:28:26.130 --> 00:28:28.450
Could you tell us a
little bit about it?

00:28:28.450 --> 00:28:29.830
Why is it cool?

00:28:29.830 --> 00:28:31.160
Why do people care about it?

00:28:31.160 --> 00:28:32.580
UMESH: Sure.

00:28:32.580 --> 00:28:36.344
Homomorphic encryption is
one of the more amazing

00:28:36.344 --> 00:28:38.510
theoretical advances in the
history of cryptography.

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:43.730
One of the things
that's challenging

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:46.080
about working with
encrypted data

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:50.640
is that if you use what's
called symmetric encryption,

00:28:50.640 --> 00:28:53.500
it's really like, if you have
the key you can see the data,

00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:56.330
and if you don't have the
key, you can't see the data.

00:28:56.330 --> 00:28:58.190
And that makes it
really difficult

00:28:58.190 --> 00:29:01.810
to do computations
between multiple parties

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:05.060
which don't fully trust each
other, because if you both have

00:29:05.060 --> 00:29:07.070
the key, then you kind
of do have to fully trust

00:29:07.070 --> 00:29:08.880
each other because you
can see all the data,

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:10.296
and if neither of
you has the key,

00:29:10.296 --> 00:29:13.810
then you can't really do
anything interesting, right?

00:29:13.810 --> 00:29:17.460
So people for a long time
have wanted this ability

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:21.260
to do what's called secure
multiparty computation,

00:29:21.260 --> 00:29:24.170
or kind of like
private computation

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:27.970
that can be done across
different parties.

00:29:27.970 --> 00:29:32.960
And basically, what homomorphic
encryption is about is saying,

00:29:32.960 --> 00:29:34.810
actually, there is
a way to encrypt

00:29:34.810 --> 00:29:38.310
data that allows you
to compute functions

00:29:38.310 --> 00:29:40.149
on the encrypted data.

00:29:40.149 --> 00:29:41.940
So it, actually, in an
interesting way kind

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:43.080
of calls back
something I mentioned

00:29:43.080 --> 00:29:45.670
earlier, which is it's possible
to encrypt stuff which you

00:29:45.670 --> 00:29:48.790
can't tell what it is, but
still manipulate it in a way

00:29:48.790 --> 00:29:52.570
that the result is predictable
with respect to the original.

00:29:52.570 --> 00:29:56.580
Fully homomorphic encryption--
the big advance there was it

00:29:56.580 --> 00:30:01.064
lets you compute, like,
any function in principle

00:30:01.064 --> 00:30:02.480
on the encrypted
data, which means

00:30:02.480 --> 00:30:06.270
you could in theory do any
computation at all just

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:08.240
on the encrypted data.

00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:11.210
Now, all that said,
despite the amazingness

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:14.080
of the theoretical
advance, we're still,

00:30:14.080 --> 00:30:16.480
I think it's fair to
say, quite a ways from it

00:30:16.480 --> 00:30:20.340
being really practical and
useful for most applications.

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:24.022
Part of the reason for that
is that it's really slow.

00:30:26.975 --> 00:30:29.490
This is as of at
least maybe 4 years

00:30:29.490 --> 00:30:32.340
ago or so when we looked
at a very rough estimate

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:34.391
of how much slower
would Google search be

00:30:34.391 --> 00:30:36.640
if we had to do it using
this fully homomorphic thing?

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:39.036
I think the answer was roughly
a billion times slower.

00:30:41.487 --> 00:30:42.320
FRANCESC: That's OK.

00:30:42.320 --> 00:30:42.840
MARK: I'd wait.

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:43.195
I'd wait.

00:30:43.195 --> 00:30:43.820
That'd be fine.

00:30:43.820 --> 00:30:46.450
UMESH: So it's, like,
slightly slower than normal.

00:30:46.450 --> 00:30:48.390
FRANCESC: Slightly slower.

00:30:48.390 --> 00:30:50.280
UMESH: So that's not
necessarily going

00:30:50.280 --> 00:30:53.070
to be the first application.

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:57.770
However, there are some
specialized scenarios

00:30:57.770 --> 00:31:02.470
where there's not a ton of
data, where you're not really

00:31:02.470 --> 00:31:07.170
latency sensitive, and where you
do have this scenario where you

00:31:07.170 --> 00:31:11.280
want two parties-- they want
to be able to compute something

00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:15.159
together, but they don't fully
trust each other to actually

00:31:15.159 --> 00:31:16.450
be able to use this technology.

00:31:16.450 --> 00:31:19.090
And we actually do have
some experimental projects

00:31:19.090 --> 00:31:21.160
which do stuff like that.

00:31:21.160 --> 00:31:24.630
A good example of the kind
of [INAUDIBLE] with this

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:28.420
is what's called secure
set intersection.

00:31:28.420 --> 00:31:34.010
That's where, like, let's say
that you have a bunch of users

00:31:34.010 --> 00:31:37.430
and I have a bunch of users
that are doing something,

00:31:37.430 --> 00:31:42.600
and we want to know which
of them are in common,

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:44.650
but neither of us wants
to reveal our full user

00:31:44.650 --> 00:31:46.330
list to the other one.

00:31:46.330 --> 00:31:48.490
So this would allow us
to compute something

00:31:48.490 --> 00:31:49.810
like that, for example.

00:31:49.810 --> 00:31:52.250
And maybe we're OK if
it takes a little while.

00:31:52.250 --> 00:31:55.200
It's not really a big
deal it takes a while.

00:31:55.200 --> 00:31:58.480
So along those lines,
actually, BigQuery

00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:00.840
has built some
experimental support

00:32:00.840 --> 00:32:04.930
for doing queries on encrypted
data, which basically lets

00:32:04.930 --> 00:32:08.660
you upload encrypted data so
that Big Query itself can't

00:32:08.660 --> 00:32:09.490
see it.

00:32:09.490 --> 00:32:11.970
Yet, Big Query is able to
run some queries for you,

00:32:11.970 --> 00:32:14.640
give you some answers
that will actually

00:32:14.640 --> 00:32:16.860
answer your query in a
way that BigQuery never

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:20.320
saw either the actual
question in some cases,

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:25.400
with some caveats, or the data
itself, which is pretty cool.

00:32:25.400 --> 00:32:28.550
It's not super scalable, but it
is something that is out there,

00:32:28.550 --> 00:32:30.430
and you can actually
play with it on GitHub.

00:32:30.430 --> 00:32:31.054
FRANCESC: Cool.

00:32:31.054 --> 00:32:33.092
That sounds like the future.

00:32:33.092 --> 00:32:34.300
That sounds very interesting.

00:32:34.300 --> 00:32:36.100
MARK: Well, we're definitely
running out of time,

00:32:36.100 --> 00:32:38.110
but just very quickly,
is there anything else

00:32:38.110 --> 00:32:39.870
we might have missed
or you need to plug,

00:32:39.870 --> 00:32:43.100
or resources people
should go look at?

00:32:43.100 --> 00:32:45.030
UMESH: I think
probably the best thing

00:32:45.030 --> 00:32:46.530
to do if you're
really interested in

00:32:46.530 --> 00:32:50.620
this topic is to check out
the encryption white paper

00:32:50.620 --> 00:32:52.150
that we recently put out.

00:32:52.150 --> 00:32:56.470
It has a ton of detail
on how this stuff works.

00:32:56.470 --> 00:32:59.372
We can also put up a link to
the encrypted BigQuery client

00:32:59.372 --> 00:33:00.830
if you're interested
to play around

00:33:00.830 --> 00:33:04.370
with homomorphic encryption.

00:33:04.370 --> 00:33:07.150
And also, stay tuned
for future episodes

00:33:07.150 --> 00:33:09.853
where we talk about a whole
bunch of other security topics.

00:33:09.853 --> 00:33:10.740
FRANCESC: Cool.

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:12.740
We'll have all of those
links on the show notes,

00:33:12.740 --> 00:33:15.570
and we'll be waiting for you to
come back to the next episodes.

00:33:15.570 --> 00:33:16.820
All right, thanks a lot, guys.

00:33:16.820 --> 00:33:17.490
It's been a pleasure.

00:33:17.490 --> 00:33:18.690
MARK: Thank you so much.

00:33:18.690 --> 00:33:22.170
Thanks again to Umesh
for joining us today.

00:33:22.170 --> 00:33:25.280
That was a great conversation
about security-- something

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:29.500
I am very glad that I don't
have to worry about as much,

00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:31.640
because that sounds
super complicated.

00:33:31.640 --> 00:33:32.950
FRANCESC: Yeah.

00:33:32.950 --> 00:33:34.840
It is very complicated,
and it's one

00:33:34.840 --> 00:33:38.460
of those things where it is
cool to try to understand it

00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:41.060
and everything, but knowing
that the responsibility

00:33:41.060 --> 00:33:43.989
of the system is in your hands--
that is, like, no, no, no, no.

00:33:43.989 --> 00:33:45.780
MARK: Yeah, it's totally
the double whammy.

00:33:45.780 --> 00:33:48.895
It's super complicated, but you
do not want to get it wrong.

00:33:48.895 --> 00:33:49.520
FRANCESC: Yeah.

00:33:49.520 --> 00:33:55.090
So very happy with all the
content of the interview.

00:33:55.090 --> 00:33:55.910
I learned a lot.

00:33:55.910 --> 00:33:59.680
And I'm actually looking forward
to next episodes on security.

00:33:59.680 --> 00:34:00.180
MARK: Yeah.

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:01.180
I want to hear more.

00:34:01.180 --> 00:34:02.730
Yeah.

00:34:02.730 --> 00:34:05.923
OK, so I think it's time to go
to the question of the week.

00:34:05.923 --> 00:34:08.280
And as we said before,
the question of the week

00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:09.239
is quite simple.

00:34:09.239 --> 00:34:12.139
OK, so I have static
content-- let's say,

00:34:12.139 --> 00:34:16.158
HTML, CSS, JavaScript--
and I want to serve it.

00:34:16.158 --> 00:34:16.949
MARK: What do I do?

00:34:16.949 --> 00:34:17.570
FRANCESC: What do we do?

00:34:17.570 --> 00:34:19.460
MARK: Yeah, so I actually
brought this one up

00:34:19.460 --> 00:34:22.719
because we recently released
a whole solution around this,

00:34:22.719 --> 00:34:24.620
which I think is
highly extensive

00:34:24.620 --> 00:34:25.927
and really, really cool.

00:34:25.927 --> 00:34:28.510
So let's go through the layers,
which are really, really cool.

00:34:28.510 --> 00:34:31.300
So say you have nothing-- don't
need to worry about anything

00:34:31.300 --> 00:34:33.060
dynamic, everything's fine.

00:34:33.060 --> 00:34:34.860
You can serve static
content right out

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:36.385
through Google Cloud Storage.

00:34:36.385 --> 00:34:37.010
FRANCESC: Cool.

00:34:37.010 --> 00:34:37.530
MARK: Which is cool.

00:34:37.530 --> 00:34:38.920
So you get edge
caching, you don't

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:41.336
need to worry about running
up servers, nothing like that.

00:34:41.336 --> 00:34:42.489
Very, very simple to use.

00:34:42.489 --> 00:34:45.199
You push it up, it
stays there-- beautiful.

00:34:45.199 --> 00:34:48.620
But it is worth noting
that if you have content

00:34:48.620 --> 00:34:51.104
that you want served over
HTTPS, unfortunately,

00:34:51.104 --> 00:34:53.020
Google Cloud Storage
won't help you with that,

00:34:53.020 --> 00:34:54.739
so you're going to have to look
at one of the other options.

00:34:54.739 --> 00:34:56.139
FRANCESC: Not yet.

00:34:56.139 --> 00:34:58.930
I'm sure that this is something
that we will add at some point,

00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:02.130
but it is important to take into
account that if you want HTTPS,

00:35:02.130 --> 00:35:05.672
Google Cloud Storage is probably
not the best place to put it.

00:35:05.672 --> 00:35:06.380
MARK: Absolutely.

00:35:06.380 --> 00:35:08.510
